From 3221cdcd07fc9191abd4f2907427a93a8276eb7c Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: lens0021 <leslie@portone.io>
Date: Thu, 24 Sep 2026 20:53:25 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] winewayland: Let a window ask to come to the front

The driver interface has pActivateWindow and winewayland does not
implement it, so a window that calls SetForegroundWindow on itself
reaches nothing. X11 sends _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW there. Wayland has
xdg-activation for the same thing.

Ask the compositor for a token, attach the last input serial and the
seat, and activate the surface when the token arrives.

Only ask when the process has just had input. Windows does not let a
background process take the foreground, and one of the conditions it
checks is whether that process received the last input event. Without
the same rule a chat client drags its window in front on every
notification, which is not something the user asked for. Track the
time of real input, keys and pointer buttons, not focus coming and
going, and let a request through for two seconds after it.

The token arrives a round trip later, so keep only the most recent
request and drop the token if the window is no longer in the
foreground by then. A stale token drags the compositor's focus back
to a window that has moved on, which cancels whatever grabbed the
input in the meantime. Tracking a popup menu is the case that showed
it. X11 has no such window because it sends _NET_ACTIVE_WINDOW on the
spot, but it does skip a window it has already asked for.
---
